INT. HOUSEBOAT - GALLEY - NIGHT

A narrow houseboat kitchen on a quiet canal. Copper pans, a ticking clock, a window onto black water and the yellow smear of a distant bridge lamp. Everything is neat. A table is set for one: a bowl of soup, a heel of bread, a folded napkin.

A WOMAN, 40s, in a gray cardigan, sits down to eat. Her hair is combed. Her posture is perfect. She tears the bread and dips it. She eats like someone who has practiced being unhurried.

A phone rings on the wall. A rotary phone, cream colored, with a long coiled cord. She lets it ring twice, wipes her fingers on the napkin, and answers.

WOMAN
Hello.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
It's me.

WOMAN
I know.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Are you alone?

WOMAN
I'm having soup.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
That's not what I asked.

WOMAN
It's what you get.

A boat horn sounds far down the canal. She turns the phone cord around her finger.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm at the lock. Twenty minutes.

WOMAN
It's a long way to the lock.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
I have the car.

WOMAN
You have a car. Good for you.

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Don't be like this.

WOMAN
Like what?

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Calm.

WOMAN
It's soup. You can be calm over soup.

She carries the phone to the table, stretching the cord to its limit, and sits. She lifts the spoon.

WOMAN (CONT'D)
Augusto. Is that you?

VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Who else would it be?

WOMAN
You never say your name.

AUGUSTO (V.O.)
(over phone)
I thought you'd know.

WOMAN
I do. I just wanted to hear it.

A pause on the line. Water laps the hull. Her free hand slides under the table. We see it open a drawer and close around the grip of a revolver, a small black thing, cold and neat. Her face does not change.

AUGUSTO (V.O.)
(over phone)
I'm sorry about Thursday.

WOMAN
So am I.

AUGUSTO (V.O.)
(over phone)
We can fix it.

WOMAN
Of course we can.

She eats a spoonful of soup, swallows, dabs her lips. The revolver stays in her lap, out of sight.

AUGUSTO (V.O.)
(over phone)
Ten minutes.

WOMAN
I'll leave the light on.

She hangs up. Sits there a long moment, looking at the black window. Her reflection looks back. Then she lifts the napkin, folds it into a perfect square, sets the gun in her lap, and waits.
